/**
 * @defgroup build Build System
 * @ingroup module_development
 * @brief Configure, build, test, package, and document ascii-chat
 */

/**
 * @page topic_build CMake Build System
 * @ingroup build
 * @brief The current CMake workflow for desktop, documentation-only, CI, and iOS builds.
 *
 * @section build_overview Overview
 *
 * ascii-chat uses CMake with Ninja as its normal generator. The root project
 * configures the native library, executable, dependencies, tests, packaging,
 * and optional web build. Use a separate build directory for each materially
 * different compiler, ABI, SDK, or static/shared dependency configuration.
 *
 * The source tree is modular: `cmake/init/` establishes options and platform
 * detection; `cmake/compiler/` owns Clang/LLVM flags and instrumentation;
 * `cmake/deps/` finds or builds third-party libraries; `cmake/targets/` assembles
 * targets; `cmake/install/` owns installation and packaging; and
 * `docs/CMakeLists.txt` is the independent Doxygen project.
 *
 * @section build_quickstart Desktop quick start
 *
 * Configure once, then build as often as needed:
 *
 * @code{.bash}
 * cmake --preset dev
 * cmake --build --preset dev
 *
 * cmake --preset debug
 * cmake --build --preset debug
 *
 * cmake --preset release
 * cmake --build --preset release
 * @endcode
 *
 * A build preset does not replace the initial configure step. Manual configuration
 * is useful for one-off combinations:
 *
 * @code{.bash}
 * cmake -S . -B build-custom -G Ninja \
 *   -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo -DBUILD_TESTS=OFF
 * cmake --build build-custom
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section build_presets Presets
 *
 * Inspect the repository's current preset names:
 * @code{.bash}
 * cmake --list-presets
 * cmake --list-presets=build
 * cmake --list-presets=test
 * @endcode
 *
 * | Preset | Intended use |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `default` | Debug baseline with tests off |
 * | `debug` | Debug with the normal diagnostic configuration |
 * | `debug-fast` | Debug symbols without sanitizer overhead |
 * | `dev` | Fast daily iteration |
 * | `tests` | Debug configuration with native tests enabled |
 * | `relwithdebinfo` | Optimized build retaining symbols |
 * | `release` | Distribution-oriented optimized build |
 * | `release-musl` | Explicit Linux musl build |
 * | `coverage` | Debug build with coverage instrumentation |
 * | `windows-debug`, `windows-release` | Windows/vcpkg configurations |
 *
 * @section build_platforms Toolchains and platforms
 *
 * Clang/LLVM and Ninja are the supported native toolchain. Windows development
 * normally uses PowerShell with LLVM and the repository presets. Linux and macOS
 * use the platform's Clang or the configured LLVM installation. Keep separate
 * build directories when switching compiler installations or sanitizer runtimes.
 * The native defer tool requires LLVM 19 or newer, its C++ development headers
 * and libraries, and libclang. The dependency installers provision these;
 * `ASCIICHAT_LLVM_DEV_ROOT` selects an existing compatible development archive.
 * On Windows the installer uses the verified LLVM 23.1.3 archive by default.
 * The optional Python defer backend locates libclang automatically, or accepts
 * `ASCIICHAT_LIBCLANG_PATH` when it cannot be discovered.
 *
 * Linux release builds can use `USE_MUSL=ON`; that path is Linux-only and needs
 * musl-gcc, GCC as its underlying compiler, and LLD. macOS uses Clang, Apple
 * frameworks, and the configured Homebrew/system dependencies. Windows release
 * builds use the vcpkg configuration and static triplet selected by the preset.
 *
 * @section build_dependencies Dependencies and cache
 *
 * The root configure step may find system packages, use vcpkg on Windows, or
 * build dependencies through the repository's CMake modules. The main cache
 * controls are `ASCIICHAT_DEPS_CACHE_ROOT`, `ASCIICHAT_DEPS_CACHE_DIR`, and the
 * derived `FETCHCONTENT_BASE_DIR`. Keep caches outside disposable build trees.
 * Do not reuse dependency artifacts across incompatible SDKs, architectures,
 * compilers, or musl/glibc configurations.
 *
 * Git submodules are not required by the documentation-only project. Native
 * configuration may initialize declared submodules when a dependency or feature
 * needs them. If initialization reports that a submodule directory is non-empty,
 * repair that specific partial checkout before retrying.
 *
 * See @ref topic_dependencies for the dependency inventory and platform notes.
 *
 * @section build_options Important options
 *
 * | Option | Effect |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `BUILD_TESTS=ON` | Build native test targets |
 * | `ASCIICHAT_ENABLE_COVERAGE=ON` | Add coverage instrumentation |
 * | `ASCIICHAT_ENABLE_ANALYZERS=ON` | Enable configured Clang-tidy/cppcheck analysis |
 * | `ASCIICHAT_BUILD_WITH_PANIC=ON` | Request panic/source instrumentation (currently incompatible with defer) |
 * | `ASCIICHAT_USE_PCH=OFF` | Disable precompiled headers |
 * | `ASCIICHAT_ENABLE_UNITY_BUILDS=ON` | Batch sources for faster builds |
 * | `USE_MUSL=ON` | Select the Linux musl path |
 * | `ASCIICHAT_ENFORCE_STATIC_RELEASE=OFF` | Permit a non-static release configuration |
 * | `ASCIICHAT_FORMAT_ONLY=ON` | Configure only formatting-related paths |
 * | `USE_CPACK=ON` | Enable package generation |
 *
 * Compiler, ABI, dependency, and target-platform options belong in a fresh build
 * directory rather than being toggled in place.
 *
 * @section build_tests Tests and CI
 *
 * @code{.bash}
 * cmake --preset tests
 * cmake --build --preset tests
 * ctest --preset unit
 * ctest --preset integration
 * ctest --preset all
 * @endcode
 *
 * GitHub Actions uses dedicated CI presets and workflows. Diagnose failures from
 * the job log instead of copying every CI cache flag into a local build.
 *
 * @section build_documentation Documentation builds
 *
 * Documentation is a separate CMake project. It does not configure the native
 * application, compile OpenSSL, initialize the full native dependency graph,
 * or require all submodules. The Doxygen theme submodule is the relevant HTML
 * styling input.
 *
 * @code{.bash}
 * cmake -S docs -B build/docs-standalone \
 *   -DASCII_CHAT_SOURCE_DIR=$PWD
 * cmake --build build/docs-standalone --target docs
 * @endcode
 *
 * HTML is written to `build/docs-standalone/share/doc/html/index.html`. The root
 * project can import the same target with `cmake --build <native-build> --target
 * docs-html`; the standalone project is preferred for docs-only work and CI.
 *
 * @section build_ios iOS prototype and Apple targets
 *
 * Pull request [#626](https://github.com/zfogg/ascii-chat/pull/626) adds the
 * current iOS simulator/device prototype. It is an in-progress platform target,
 * not a desktop executable. CMake builds a static `libasciichat.a`-based app and
 * keeps desktop-only executables, tests, TTY setup, and unsupported QUIC paths
 * out of the iOS SDK build.
 *
 * The prototype includes iOS platform detection, arm64 device and configurable
 * simulator builds, a SwiftUI target named `ascii-chat-ios-app`, an AVFoundation
 * camera bridge, an AVAudioEngine-backed PortAudio-compatible audio shim, a C
 * render bridge, native mode/lifecycle state, background audio handling, and an
 * XCFramework packaging helper. The Intel simulator has no camera; physical
 * camera and microphone validation remains device work.
 *
 * @subsection build_ios_simulator Simulator
 *
 * Run on macOS with Xcode and the iOS simulator SDK:
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./scripts/build-ios-simulator.sh build-ios-sim
 * IOS_SIM_ARCH=arm64 ./scripts/build-ios-simulator.sh build-ios-sim-arm64
 * @endcode
 *
 * The helper validates `IOS_SIM_ARCH` (`x86_64` or `arm64`), configures
 * `BUILD_IOS_SIM=ON`, and builds `ascii-chat-ios-app`.
 *
 * @subsection build_ios_device Device
 *
 * A device build requires the iPhoneOS SDK and a fresh build directory:
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./scripts/build-ios-device.sh build-ios
 * @endcode
 *
 * The helper checks `xcrun --sdk iphoneos --show-sdk-path` and rejects a directory
 * previously configured for the simulator.
 *
 * @subsection build_ios_xcframework XCFramework
 *
 * Build both archives and package them with public headers:
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./scripts/build-ios-simulator.sh build-ios-sim
 * ./scripts/build-ios-device.sh build-ios
 * ./scripts/package-ios-xcframework.sh \
 *   build-ios/lib/libasciichat.a \
 *   build-ios-sim/lib/libasciichat.a \
 *   build/asciichat.xcframework
 * @endcode
 *
 * The packager refuses to overwrite an existing output, stages public headers plus
 * the required Sokol headers, and invokes `xcodebuild -create-xcframework`.
 *
 * @section build_targets Useful targets
 *
 * | Target | Purpose |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | `ascii-chat` | Desktop executable |
 * | `ascii-chat-static-lib` | Static native library |
 * | `ascii-chat-ios-app` | SwiftUI iOS prototype app |
 * | `tests` | Native test aggregation target |
 * | `docs-html` | Generate HTML documentation |
 * | `docs-open` | Generate docs and print the local index path |
 * | `format` | Format configured sources |
 * | `package` | Generate available CPack packages |
 *
 * @section build_install Packaging and installation
 *
 * @code{.bash}
 * cmake --build build/dev
 * cmake --install build/dev --prefix $HOME/.local
 * cmake --build build/dev --target package
 * @endcode
 *
 * CPack output depends on host tools available during configuration. Inspect the
 * configure summary instead of assuming every host produces DEB, RPM, PKG, or NSIS.
 *
 * @section build_troubleshooting Troubleshooting
 *
 * - **Wrong compiler or SDK:** start with a fresh build directory and inspect `CMakeCache.txt`.
 * - **Submodule failure:** repair the reported partial submodule checkout specifically.
 * - **Unexpected dependency rebuild:** check cache root and target ABI/SDK.
 * - **iOS probes target the host:** use the iOS helper so FFmpeg and ICU probes receive the selected SDK.
 * - **Swift receives Clang flags:** keep language-specific options scoped to C/C++ targets.
 *
 * @see CMakeLists.txt
 * @see CMakePresets.json
 * @see docs/CMakeLists.txt
 * @see topic_dependencies
 * @see topic_testing
 */
